"Count-Min sketch.The CM sketch was first described by Cormode and Muthukrishnan [ 27,28]. It uses hash functions to map events to frequencies, but unlike a hash table uses only sub-linear space, at the expense of overcounting some events due to collisions. Count–min sketch is an alternative to count sketch [ 22] and AMS sketch [ 5] and can be considered an implementation of a counting Bloom filter [ 38,45] or multistage-filter [ 27]. Over the years multiple variations of min-count sketches have been proposed in the literature [ 41,58,68,72,85] improving the estimation or the performance under different settings. While CM sketches have been thoroughly studied there is still room for improvement. For example, the random hashing procedure may induce substantial uncertainty in the estimation, especially for low-frequency tokens. Furthermore, it is often the case that there exists an a priori knowledge on the data, and therefore it may be desirable to incorporate such a knowledge into the estimates Recently, machine learning is used to resolve these issues. More specifically, machine learning techniques [ 1,19,32,33,50,65,91] are used to learn either the hash functions or a partition of the elements into two groups (based on their frequency) to improve the estimations or the performance compared to the traditional CM sketch. Interestingly, partitioned-learned CM sketches [ 50,65] learn"

This paragraph:
- Describes the Count-Min sketch (CM sketch) and its history, properties, and variants.
- It reviews existing work on CM sketches and points out limitations (uncertainty for low-frequency tokens, the possibility of incorporating a priori knowledge).
- It mentions that machine learning techniques are being used to resolve these issues (by learning hash functions or partitioning elements).

This segment is primarily a literature review. It does not introduce any new method, algorithm, or finding. It summarizes existing knowledge and points out gaps. The new knowledge here is the observation that machine learning can be used to address the limitations, but the segment does not present any novel method or finding of its own. It is repackaging existing knowledge (from the literature) and the gaps it points out are known in the field.

Therefore, this segment does not produce new knowledge. It is a summary of prior work and the gaps that have been identified in the literature. So it should get a low score.

